The '''2024–25 season''' was the 106th season in the history of '''[[Valencia CF]]''', and the club's 38th consecutive season in [[La Liga]]. In addition to the [[2024–25 La Liga|domestic league]], the club participated in the [[2024–25 Copa del Rey|Copa del Rey]].

== Summary ==
Head coach [[Rubén Baraja]]'s tenure was prolonged for another season on 19 June, extending his contract to 2026.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://onefootball.com/en/news/ruben-baraja-im-happy-and-above-all-proud-to-be-able-to-continue-fulfilling-my-dream-of-being-coach-of-valencia-cf-39646921|title=Rubén Baraja: "I'm happy and, above all, proud to be able to continue fulfilling my dream of being coach of Valencia CF"|date=11 July 2024|website=OneFootball}}</ref> On 23 December 2024, having achieved only two wins during [[2024–25 La Liga|the season]], Baraja was sacked.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.valenciacf.com/comunicado-oficial-ruben-baraja1|title=Comunicado oficial {{!}} Rubén Baraja|trans-title=Official announcement {{!}} Rubén Baraja|publisher=Valencia CF|language=es|date=23 December 2024|access-date=15 August 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.ultimahora.es/deportes/futbol/2024/12/23/2296267/valencia-prescinde-ruben-baraja-tras-partidos-solo-dos-victorias.html|title=El Valencia destituye a Rubén Baraja|trans-title=Valencia dismiss Rubén Baraja|newspaper=[[Última Hora (Spain)|Última Hora]]|last=Lizón|first=Javier|language=es|date=23 December 2024|access-date=15 August 2025}}</ref> The following day, former Valencia youth player Carlos Corberán signed a 3-year deal to become the new head coach.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.mundodeportivo.com/us/en/20241225/711723/carlos-corberan-appointed-as-valencia-s-new-coach.html |title=Carlos Corberán appointed as Valencia’s new coach |publisher=Mundo Deportivo |date=25 December 2024 }}</ref>

